1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
EdgeGuard: worker–forklift collision prediction on oneM2M and ETSI MEC.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Why: forklift accidents, and alarms that make sites choose between safety and productivity.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Predict the collision course on the MEC edge, and tell each person WAIT, STEP ASIDE or GO.

4
00:00:39,000 --> 00:01:05,000
How we built it: every collision traced, fixed and tested again.

5
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
A MEC host per zone, oneM2M CSEs, and the EdgeGuard MEC app.

6
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,500
Live demo.

7
00:01:15,500 --> 00:02:00,500
WAIT until the forklift has passed, then GO; STEP ASIDE in the lane; two forklifts.

8
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:03,000
Firmware in the loop.

9
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:19,667
The ESP32 firmware against a real oneM2M CSE, a phone as the worker tag.

10
00:02:19,667 --> 00:02:22,167
Mobility through ETSI MEC.

11
00:02:22,167 --> 00:02:35,667
A MEC zone event moves the forklift's twin to zone B's CSE.

12
00:02:35,667 --> 00:02:42,667
As safe as the wide alarm, at twice the throughput.

13
00:02:42,667 --> 00:02:49,667
On bad days: 30 collisions to 1, and no moving truck touching anyone.

14
00:02:49,667 --> 00:02:55,667
Thank you.

